@TITLE@ QilinRecomp for Linux (64-bit)
==================================

Raspberry Pi: this needs the 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS. On the 32-bit one,
"Play @TITLE@" can't start (the system says it can't execute it, or that
the file isn't found). In a terminal, "getconf LONG_BIT" says 64 on a
64-bit system.

APB - All Points Bulletin (@STUDIO@), recompiled to run
natively: the arcade board's two processors' programs translated into
C++ and compiled for your computer, checked against MAME's processor
cores, drawn with Vulkan.

No game data is included. You need your own copy of the ROMs:
@RELEASE@ (136051-1101.5t to 136051-7129.6n),
unzipped into a folder of their own.
MAME's apb.zip is that set zipped: unzip it first.


Playing
-------
1. Unpack the download anywhere you can write to (your home folder is
   fine): double-click it in your file manager, or in a terminal
   "tar xzf @PACKAGE@-*.tar.gz".
   Building the game needs about 1 GB free.
2. Open the @PACKAGE@ folder and double-click "Play @TITLE@"
   (if your file manager asks, choose Execute or Run). From a terminal:
   ./"Play @TITLE@"
3. Click "Choose your ROM set" and pick the folder holding the ROM files,
   or drag the folder onto the window. (Where the desktop has no folder
   chooser, type or paste the folder's path.) It tells you straight away
   if files are missing or damaged, or if it is another revision of APB.
   ROM file names are matched exactly, in lower case, as MAME has them.
4. Wait for the first build: a few minutes on a desktop PC. Your ROMs
   never leave your computer.
5. Click Play. The window hides while the game runs. "Add to the
   applications menu" puts @TITLE@ in your desktop's menu.

After that, Play @TITLE@ remembers your ROM folder (even in a new download
folder), and Play starts the game straight away. After an update it builds
the game again first, recompiling only what changed.

The game's output is in work/run.log and work/run-errors.log, and the
build's in work/build.log. Send them when reporting a problem.

Needs: a 64-bit Linux on x86-64 (the linux-x86_64 download) or ARM64 (the
linux-arm64 download) with glibc 2.35 or later (Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12,
Fedora 36, Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm or later), and a graphics driver with
Vulkan 1.3: Mesa's Vulkan drivers (on Debian and Ubuntu, the
mesa-vulkan-drivers package) or NVIDIA's. Before building, Play @TITLE@
checks that there is one and says what to install if not. Updates use
curl (or wget) and tar, which nearly every Linux has.


Raspberry Pi
------------
- The 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS (see the top of this file), on a Raspberry Pi
  4 or 5 with 2 GB of memory or more. Use the linux-arm64 download.
- Vulkan 1.3 for the Pi's graphics arrived with Mesa 24.3: Raspberry Pi OS
  Trixie (October 2025) or later has it. On an older Raspberry Pi OS, Play
  @TITLE@ says so before building: update Raspberry Pi OS.
- The first build compiles the whole game on the Pi itself, so it takes a
  while: much longer than on a desktop PC, and longer on a Pi 4 than a Pi
  5, and on a 2 GB Pi, which compiles one file at a time. Leave it
  running. Later builds take compiled files from the cache.
- "Add to the applications menu" (once the game is built) puts @TITLE@ in
  the Raspberry Pi menu, under Games.


Controls
--------
Keyboard: arrows steer and accelerate; Left Ctrl, Left Alt, Space are the
siren/start, fire and alternate siren; 5 inserts a coin. Gamepad: left stick
or d-pad steers, right trigger or d-pad up accelerates, A or Start
starts/sirens, B fires, X is alternate siren, Back inserts a coin.

F1 or Esc (gamepad: Back + Start together): the settings menu (Page Up /
Page Down or LB / RB change its pages): wheels, pedals, buttons, the
picture (presets, size, a CRT look, smoothed pixel art), full screen, the
volume. F3: frame times. F11 or Alt+Enter: full screen. Wheels: a
direct-drive (free-turning) wheel, a mouse or a spinner; see the Controls
page.

Settings and high scores are kept in ~/.local/share/qilin/@GAME@ (or
$XDG_DATA_HOME/qilin/@GAME@), with the ROM folder you chose and the build's
cache of compiled files (build-cache).

Checking a wheel and performance: once the game is built, start it from
the download's folder with an input recording, performance timings and a
controller report, play for a couple of minutes and quit normally:
  work/@EXE_LINUX@ --record check.qir --profile performance.txt \
      --controller-report controllers.txt
controllers.txt includes serials and device paths; the recording includes
the starting high scores and settings, but no ROMs.


What's in here
--------------
Play @TITLE@            chooses your ROMs, builds the game (once), starts it
kit/qilin           the recompiler: turns the ROMs' programs into C++
kit/lib, include    the prebuilt rest of the game: the board, video, sound,
                    the window and controls (SDL3), the menu (Dear ImGui)
kit/toolchain/zig   zig, a C++ compiler (Clang, with its own libc++)
kit/icon.png        the menu entry's icon
kit/files.txt       every file this release holds (see below)
work/               made when building: the game (@EXE_LINUX@), built from your
                    ROMs, the recompiled code, the build's files and the logs
LICENSES/           the licences of everything included

zig keeps its own cache of compiled libraries in ~/.cache/zig.

Source, changes and issues: https://github.com/Qilin1066/ApbArcade--QilinRecomp


Updates and recovery
--------------------
"Check for updates" in Play @TITLE@ asks GitHub for a newer release (it
sends nothing about you or your computer). If there is one, it downloads
it, unpacks it beside this folder and swaps in the new kit and launcher,
then starts again and builds the game for the new version. If anything
fails on the way, the old version is left as it was. You can also unpack a
new download anywhere, even over this folder: it finds your ROM folder and
the cache by itself, and at its first start it removes files the new kit
no longer has (anything in kit/ or LICENSES/ that kit/files.txt doesn't
name). Your settings and high scores are never in this folder, so nothing
of yours is touched.

The game is compiled for this computer's processor; moved to another
computer, it is built again. Compiled files come from the cache when
nothing they're made from has changed. A failed build leaves the working
game as it was; the one before a new build is kept as
work/@EXE_LINUX@.previous until the new one has run and closed normally.
"Build again from scratch" compiles everything anew.
